 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N \cdot P)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels and $P$ is the number of samples per ray. Early ray termination reduces the average $P$ for opaque regions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(V)$, where $V$ is the volume of the dataset stored in 3D texture memory. Up to four data values per voxel may occupy 32 bits of graphics memory [S22, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity**: Not given in the sources.
+*   **Memory Complexity**: Not given in the sources.
 *   **Performance**: Limited by pixel fill rate and texture bandwidth. On mobile devices, moderate datasets (64×64×64) can achieve ~2.40 FPS [S10]. Loading new data from disk causes noticeable delay, but runtime interactions (navigation, TF changes) are typically smooth [S21, unofficial].
 *   **Strengths**: Preserves all data information; no preprocessing (isosurface extraction) required; handles transparent and amorphous data well [S11, unofficial][S17, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations**: Computationally expensive for large volumes; sensitive to transfer function choice; aliasing artifacts if undersampled [S5, unofficial][S25, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations**: Computationally expensive for large volumes [G]. Sensitive to transfer function choice [S7, unofficial]. Prone to aliasing artifacts if undersampled [S5, unofficial][S25, unofficial].
 *   **When to Use**: Rendering amorphous data (clouds, fluids), medical imaging with transparent tissues, or when full internal structure visibility is needed [S2, unofficial][S10].
 *   **When Not to Use**: When explicit surface geometry is required (use [Indirect Volume Rendering](3314-indirect-volume-rendering.md)), or when rendering unstructured data on GPU without specialized acceleration structures [S25, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Direct Volume Rendering was introduced in the late 1980s. Tuy and Tuy (1984) presented one of the earliest techniques using ray casting for iso-surfaces [S1, unofficial]. Levoy (1988) and Drebin, Carpenter, and Hanrahan (1988) provided foundational improvements for direct volume rendering, establishing the ray-casting pipeline and front-to-back compositing [S1, unofficial]. The method has since been widely adopted in medical imaging, scientific visualization, and mobile platforms using OpenGL ES and WebGL [S10].
